    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d2b1ec9233a4c7e88aa395df7193bf6ff9b1049f5d1e9bae124a5ac1a05081b4",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The two sections give complementary transfer mechanisms for constructed functions. Hybrid universality enlarges the controlled input by steering function values and latent torus phases with the same shift, so coefficients, products, and polynomial combinations can be synchronized. Composite universality instead pulls a desired output target back to an admissible zero-free input and uses local Holder stability to push ordinary input approximation forward through an operator.",
